{"name":"Brisket","hex":"#6e4534","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Brisket","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["The character Bridget from the video game series Guilty Gear."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Brisket"}],"entries":[{"title":"Brisket","blocks":[{"text":"Brisket is a primal cut of meat from the breast or lower chest of beef or veal. The beef brisket is one of the nine beef primal cuts, though the definition of the cut differs internationally. The brisket muscles include the superficial and deep pectorals. As cattle do not have collar bones, these muscles support about 60% of the body weight of standing or moving cattle. This requires a significant amount of connective tissue, so the resulting meat must be cooked correctly to tenderise it. The cut overlies the sternum, ribs, and connecting costal cartilages."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Brisketphoto.jpg/330px-Brisketphoto.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisket","wikidata":"Q1324342"},{"title":"Brisket (Jewish dish)","blocks":[{"text":"Brisket is a popular Ashkenazi Jewish dish of braised beef brisket, served hot and traditionally accompanied by potato or other non-dairy kugel, latkes, and often preceded by matzo ball soup. It is commonly served for Jewish holidays such as Hanukkah, Passover, Rosh Hashanah, and Shabbat. It is commonly found in Jewish communities worldwide, though it is most commonly associated with Jews in the United States, where it has been considered the most important and iconic Jewish main course since the early 20th century."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a6/Brisket_1.jpg/330px-Brisket_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisket_(Jewish_dish)","wikidata":"Q85748980"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Dutch_Beef_cuts.png/500px-Dutch_Beef_cuts.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Dutch_Beef_cuts.png/500px-Dutch_Beef_cuts.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Dutch cuts of beef including the brisket.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADutch_Beef_cuts.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Kansas_City_brisket_and_burnt_ends_dinner.png/500px-Kansas_City_brisket_and_burnt_ends_dinner.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Kansas_City_brisket_and_burnt_ends_dinner.png/1280px-Kansas_City_brisket_and_burnt_ends_dinner.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Kansas_City_brisket_and_burnt_ends_dinner.png/500px-Kansas_City_brisket_and_burnt_ends_dinner.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Kansas City-style beef brisket and burnt ends","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKansas_City_brisket_and_burnt_ends_dinner.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Beefbrisketjf.JPG/500px-Beefbrisketjf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Beefbrisketjf.JPG/1280px-Beefbrisketjf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Beefbrisketjf.JPG/500px-Beefbrisketjf.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Beef brisket noodles (Philippines)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABeefbrisketjf.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikip